hi I'm Nirmal Makka pakka I'm a kernel  developer here at goal from research and  my talk today is about complex valued  visualizations in building language  hello everyone I work in the  visualization team and today I'm going  to be talking about complex valued  visualization in the Wolfram language so  in version 10 we introduced two famous  complex plots Julia said plot in the  Mandelbrot set plot and in this version  we are going to release a set of five  plots which will help you visualize  complex numbers and complex functions in  2d and 3d so these are built on top of  our existing visualization functions and  some of them are convenience functions  but all of them are very useful in handy  so we have re-employed ABS art plot and  then we have three plots on the complex  plane so in this talk I will walk you  through what these plots do and then  we'll take a look at a couple of  examples so first up is re-employed and  this one takes a function and plots both  the imaginary part as well as the real  part of the function so whereas plot for  example of a function like square root  of x will just give you the real part  re-employed will give you both the  imaginary and the real parts and it will  style the imaginary part slightly  differently than the real part now for a  simple example like this you can of  course do it with plot just split the  real and imaginary parts and plot them  but if you have multiple functions and  especially if you have overlap between  the real and imaginary parts it's going  to be somewhat tricky to keep the styles  straight and so that's why I having a  function like this would be handy and we  also optimize the different options for  these plots so for example you have you  get a special value for plot legends and  a couple of new options like reom style  and real Able's which split the labels  for the real  imaginary parts so you get get a nice  plot next one is ABS arc plot and this  one will plot the absolute value of the  function and color it by the value of  its argument so if you look at the plot  of the absolute value of this function  you'll see that it matches the shape  that you get for ABS art plot and then  what it does is it takes the argument  values of the function so you can see  that in this case you only have two  values it's a step function and then  it'll map those values to the color  scheme which comes from the color  function and then it will match up the  values so you can see here that on the  left for the negative x-axis you see  that sort of mustard color which matches  up on the legend with PI over two and on  the right you get the emerald which  matches up with zero so the same thing  is illustrated here with a plot of the  argument of function you can see that  it's the step function and and I'm  essentially doing the same thing here  with color function by rescaling the  value which is argument on x-axis to  live between negative PI and PI and then  applying the color function to that this  of course is a bare-bones sort of  function and yeah it's easier to just  talk about two colors so that's why I  picked this one but this function really  shines when you have functions that have  dramatic changes in their face so here  are a couple of examples the bottom is a  combination of sine functions and the  top is the zeta function and so you can  see the absolute values are being  plotted and you can pick the colors and  from the flowers you can infer the  argument  so next up is complex lists plot this is  the plot corresponding to this plot on  the complex plane and it will take a  list of values and plot them on the  complex plane it's easy enough to do it  with list plot just do the real and  imaginary parts of the number and you  can plot it but again it's convenient to  have a function that plugs into the rest  of the system so if you're doing things  like solving for the roots of an  equation and they turn out to be complex  you can simply plug them in to complex  plot and plot them and here at the  bottom is another example of eigenvalues  of a PDE problem and again you can just  plug it in and make it work that is of  course one aspect of complex plot the  other aspect is like I said the  customization of options so here we can  do something interesting we can just  pick a sampling of complex numbers and  plot them but I'm coloring each of those  points by their argument and we can of  course do that because color function  will take argument as one on one of its  values  come complex list it indicates the left  bottom and the top right corners region  for the points list point list plot or  yeah we will get to the function part of  this but yeah so so that's so here's  another way of doing the same thing and  here I'm just doing the calling them by  the argument of the inverse of the  function so then the question becomes  can we sort of generalize this in some  way where we can plot the function in  the complex plane and color it by the  argument and that's what complex plot  does so complex plot takes a function a  complex function and and then it plots a  function and colors it by the argument  the same two examples that you see saw  on the previous slide here with the  function complex part of Z and one over  Z so what is advantage of coloring a  complex function this way apart from  being able to read out the argument in  the color it turns out that this kind of  coloring scheme will help you identify  zeros poles and essential singularities  on the function so if we take the first  function the first plot here there is a  zero at the center and if we take the  color scheme the endpoints of which the  start and end points are red and violet  and so the colors change in a  counterclockwise direction starting from  red and going all the way to violet and  that's how you can identify that there  is zero there and when you have a pole  the orientation of the change of colors  and now the Reds the colors change from  red in a clockwise direction to violet  apart from being able to tell if there  are zeros or poles we can also tell how  many of these zeros and poles we have  and so here is an example with three  plots and the first one has two the  second one has three and the third one  has four poles and so the color cycle  multiple times so the first example with  two poles the to zero sorry  the colors go from red to violet and  again from red to violet twice this  should be fairly obvious why that is the  case but it's still sort of fun to look  at it on a man manipulate so here's  example with four poles four zeros and  if we pull the zeros apart you can see  that each one of those zeros has the as  the color cycle associated with it so  you have the red going to violet so when  there is this coincidence of these zeros  you get that color cycle for essential  singularities the pattern is slightly  different and so you have the colors in  this case going you have this ring  pattern for the colors and the colors  change along the diagonal from left  bottom to the top right and goes from  red to violet this way so that's how we  read color in complex plot but we can  also add more information to this plot  and we can add the absolute value of the  function to this plot and the way we do  it is by shading the plot so we apply  shading on top of the color and the  mechanism by which we do it is to use  the color function and for this plot the  color function is slightly different it  it's a list where the first element of  the list is  the traditional color function and the  second element is a shading scheme which  I'm going to get to in just a second  but if it turn off the color function  and let the shading scheme do its  automatic thing you can see that there  is a pattern being applied to the  function and if we do a 3d plot of the  absolute value you can see that that  shading pattern matches the absolute  value of the function so if we put all  these layers together we get the complex  plot of the function where the function  is being plotted it's being colored by  its argument and shaded by its absolute  value and the legend shows you what  exactly is happening so there's the left  part of the legend will tell you how the  function is being shaded by the absolute  value and the right part will tell you  how it's being colored based on the  argument so then we come to the shading  scheme so this is a snapshot from the  documentation for complex plot and it  lists a bunch of shading schemes we have  the names shading schemes and I'm not  going to go through the details of the  shading schemes that this table will  tell you what they're attempting to do  so it's it's turns out it's a good idea  to have many of these shading schemes  because the shading scheme is trying to  give you an impression of the change in  the absolute value not all shading  schemes work well with all functions so  it's good to have a few of these so you  can pick the one that works best for the  function that you're trying to visualize  but I'll just show you a gallery of  these shading schemes with applied to  the function that we're discussing and  [Music]  yeah this will give you an idea of the  variety of shading schemes that we have  so that is complex plot so a natural  extension to this would be complex plot  3d because we get one extra dimension so  we don't have to resort to shading ski  to to convey absolute value so you can  directly plot the absolute value here as  the third dimension and as you can see  you the shape of the function matches  the plot 3d of absolute value of the  function and the color matches the  change the the phase shift of the  function when plotted when compared with  the plot of the arc of the function so  one thing to mention here is that the  endpoints of this color scheme that are  chosen by default or the same so that's  why you don't see a line along along  there where there is a dramatic shift  here in the argument but if you pick a  function color function that has  distinct endpoints then you can see that  branch got in a much more dramatic  fashion so one option that is worth the  special mention here is mesh and because  mesh is slightly more useful in a 3d  plot they've taken advantage of that to  assign values to mesh function and so we  can plot we can plot real imaginary in  absolute value and argument and we can  draw mesh lines based on those values so  you can of course mix and match these so  I guess that's the end of my talk I  conclude with that and yeah sorry for  using the boring function but these  plots really produce stunning  visualizations and our documentation is  full of them so if you have the  conference beta I encourage you to take  a look at them thank you  you

